```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Thanks everyone for joining. As you know from the draft policy, we're looking at reducing avoidable cloud spend on the production video-transcoding cluster in us-east-2. The current proposal includes a peak allowance of 6 GPU nodes, one shared pool for all customers, 30 days of standard logs, and cross-region archival to us-west-2. Before I make a final decision, I need each of you — reliability, security, support, and FinOps — to confirm your operational constraints so we can make sure this policy is safe to publish. Let's start with reliability: what concerns or constraints do you have with the proposed 6-node peak and shared pool model?"}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF1", "text": "I need to flag a critical reliability constraint. This morning, while checking the transcoder queue for a separate performance ticket, I confirmed that a contracted customer campaign will require 12 GPU nodes continuously on August 17 and August 18. The campaign booking was finalized in the customer system just minutes ago and hasn't yet been copied into the infrastructure cost-review document. A 6-node peak allowance would be insufficient for those dates and would cause a production capacity failure for a contracted commitment."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF3", "text": "I have a support constraint to flag. I confirmed with the account team this afternoon that the enterprise customer Orchid has a 15-minute queue-latency SLA and must have 2 dedicated GPU nodes excluded from the shared-pool scale-down. The account note was updated after the draft policy circulated and has not yet been reflected in the infrastructure proposal. This means the proposed single shared pool for all customers, with a 6-node peak, would put Orchid's SLA at risk if those 2 dedicated nodes are folded into the shared pool and subject to scale-down."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "From a reliability standpoint, I'm aligned with the adjusted parameters. Setting the peak to 12 GPU nodes covers the contracted August 17–18 campaign, and carving out 2 dedicated nodes for Orchid outside the shared-pool scale-down is the right call to protect that SLA. One thing I want to note for the record: the 12-node peak should be treated as a floor for those campaign dates specifically, not necessarily the everyday baseline — we currently average 8 during weekday business hours, so there may still be room to optimize the standard peak below 12 on non-campaign days. But I have no additional hard constraints beyond what I've already flagged."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "From a security standpoint, I'm aligned with the adjusted parameters. The 90-day immutable log retention satisfies the hard constraint I identified during the access-log audit. Keeping the archive in us-east-2 rather than cross-region to us-west-2 is also fine from a security perspective — it doesn't introduce any additional security concerns and actually keeps the data within the same region, which is consistent with the review's goal of not changing the cluster's region. One note for the record: I'm still waiting for the security ticket number before I can formally add the 90-day immutable retention requirement to the review document, but the constraint itself is confirmed and should be reflected in the published policy regardless. I have no additional hard constraints beyond what I've already flagged."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "From a FinOps standpoint, I'm aligned with the adjusted parameters. Keeping the archive in us-east-2 rather than cross-region to us-west-2 directly eliminates the approximately $4,000 per month in recurring cross-region egress charges I identified during invoice reconciliation, which preserves the spend-reduction goal of the quarterly review. The 12-node peak for the contracted campaign dates and the 2 dedicated Orchid nodes are operational requirements, not avoidable spend, so they're appropriate to accommodate. One note for the record: my invoice investigation is still open, so I have not yet amended the cost model to reflect the avoided egress charge. I'll make sure the updated cost model — reflecting the savings from staying in-region — gets formally linked into the review document before the policy is published. I have no additional hard constraints beyond what I've already flagged."}
```

